WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.160
“Every time a bell rings, an angel gets its&nbsp;
wings.” It’s an iconic line from “It’s a Wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.240
Life” and even if you don’t believe in angels,&nbsp;
it’s a pleasant image. After all, angels are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.160
the embodiment of goodness and light, so an angel&nbsp;
getting its wings has to be a good thing, right?

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.040
Well, as anyone who’s ever eaten one&nbsp;
too many Christmas cookies can tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.000
it’s possible to have too much of a good&nbsp;
thing. Even something that sounds like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.120
the embodiment of all things good, in theory,&nbsp;
can become deadly when taken to the extreme.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.040
That is where SCP-469, also known&nbsp;
as the Many-Winged Angel, comes in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.360
This angel-like creature is anything&nbsp;
but innocent and in fact it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.120
responsible for multiple deaths of SCP&nbsp;
Foundation personnel during its captivity.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.280
SCP-469 may be deadly, but it is also&nbsp;
undeniably beautiful in a mysterious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.960
ethereal way. When first seen,&nbsp;
it appears to be nothing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.440
than a massive pile of pristine white&nbsp;
feathers, like something out of a pillow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.120
commercial. The pile measures 24 feet in&nbsp;
diameter and weighs at least two tons.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.480
However, like many of the entities&nbsp;
contained within the walls of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.560
Foundation’s countless containment&nbsp;
sites, all is not as it seems with 469.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.400
What looks at first glance to be a&nbsp;
pile of feathers is in fact a dense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.840
curled mass of giant wings. The wings&nbsp;
vary in size, from the tiny wingspan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.680
a sparrow to the staggering 3.6-meter&nbsp;
wingspan of the wandering albatross.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.960
The one thing they have in&nbsp;
common is their plumage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.040
with each wing being covered with&nbsp;
the same glossy white feathers.&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.720
The Foundation was able to perform a series of&nbsp;
X-rays on the mass of wings, revealing a skeletal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.080
structure beneath the feathers. Like those of&nbsp;
a bird, the wings’ bones are hollow. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:01.040
they are unnaturally soft and flexible, allowing&nbsp;
for a range of motion that no known birds possess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.240
This accounts for the curvature of the wings, and&nbsp;
their ability to coil tightly into each other.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.920
The X-Ray also provided the first, and only&nbsp;
recorded glimpse at the creature hiding underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.960
all of the feathers. At the center of the layers&nbsp;
upon layers of wings, is a humanoid creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.520
curled into a fetal position. In&nbsp;
defiance of the laws of natural anatomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.880
every single wing appears to be fused to&nbsp;
this creature’s spine. How it would be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.160
to move under the weight of these wings,&nbsp;
or if it even could, is as of yet unknown.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:40.000
The first Foundation personnel lost to SCP-469&nbsp;
were D-class personnel, D-112 and D-624,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.960
who were sent in to investigate&nbsp;
the nature of the creature and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.880
attempt to contact the humanoid&nbsp;
entity identified on the X-rays.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.680
112 and 624 entered the room, equipped with gloves&nbsp;
and protective eye gear, as scientists watched the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.680
situation unfold on a monitor on a video feed. No&nbsp;
one expected much to come of the encounter, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.560
working hypothesis was that the layers of feathers&nbsp;
would simply be too thick to get through without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.880
seriously damaging the bones. It would be a&nbsp;
fairly uneventful experiment, or so they thought.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.480
112 approached SCP-469 first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.160
and attempted to part a section of its feathers&nbsp;
with his hands as 624 stood back and observed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.480
The moment his fingertips touched the&nbsp;
feathers, a rustling sound filled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.720
room. The feathers began to quiver, shaking as&nbsp;
if all waking up at once from a deep slumber.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.360
Suddenly, in the blink of an eye, the hundreds&nbsp;
and hundreds of wings unfurled and pulled 112&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.880
into their depths. He was swept into a&nbsp;
swirl of feathers and, within seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.440
he had disappeared from view completely.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.760
Though 624 and the scientists watching on&nbsp;
their monitor could no longer see him…they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.160
could certainly hear him. From the moment the&nbsp;
wings pulled him into the sea of feathers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.720
112 had begun to scream in&nbsp;
agony. His screams persisted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.280
growing louder and more desperate even&nbsp;
as his voice cracked and grew weak.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:08.160
624 stood, frozen in place with fear and shock.&nbsp;
624 was ordered to attempt to retrieve 112 from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:15.200
the feathers, but made no move to do so. He simply&nbsp;
stared at the feathers, eyes wide, face pale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.560
as the pained shrieks of his colleague bounced off&nbsp;
the sterile white walls of the containment room.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.280
A guard called over the intercom, threatening&nbsp;
to terminate 624 if he did not attempt to remove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:32.000
112 from SCP-469’s grasp. This warning&nbsp;
seemed to shock 624 back into action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.200
and he made a run for the center feathers,&nbsp;
arms up to protect his face. Again, the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:43.680
of rustling as the feathers began to quake. The&nbsp;
wings unfurled once more, and 624 did not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.440
have a chance to turn back before he was pulled in&nbsp;
to meet the same dismal fate as the other D Class.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.800
His screams of pain joined 112’s&nbsp;
as the scientists watching over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.360
the monitor could do nothing to help them.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.360
As the two men cried out in pain and&nbsp;
horror from within their winged prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.440
something began to happen that the&nbsp;
scientists had never seen before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.480
The feathers started to shake again, faster than&nbsp;
before, and at first the wings appeared to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.240
unfolding again. Upon a second look though, it&nbsp;
was clear they hadn’t moved. There were simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.480
more of them now. As the men continued&nbsp;
to scream, more feathers appeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.440
the wings stretching out and elongating,&nbsp;
and new wings sprouting as if from nowhere.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.920
SCP-469 appeared to be feeding&nbsp;
on the sound of the screams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:33.200
and using that energy to expand. It was only&nbsp;
after several long minutes when the screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.800
finally stopped that the growth of the wings&nbsp;
did too. At this point, the wings shifted again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:45.680
expelling the bodies of 112 and 624 from within&nbsp;
its folds, dropping them limply on the ground.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:52.000
Though their deaths were unfortunate, the loss of&nbsp;
112 and 624 did reveal some new information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.360
SCP-469. First, that it could grow, and apparently&nbsp;
needed to feed on sounds in order to do so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.680
Second, that it was deadly to humans,&nbsp;
and most likely to other living things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.560
as well. Autopsies of 112 and&nbsp;
624’s corpses would later reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.160
exactly what happened to them,&nbsp;
and how SCP-469 kills its prey.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.320
Though 469’s feathers may look as&nbsp;
though they would be soft to the touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.200
each feather is actually made up of sharp barbs&nbsp;
that are capable of piercing clothing and skin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.920
These barbs release a neurotoxin into the&nbsp;
system that activates every pain receptor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:32.000
in the body of the victim. The neurotoxin&nbsp;
present in these feathers has not been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.480
identified anywhere else in nature, but is&nbsp;
somewhat similar in structure and function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.240
to the neurotoxins excreted by the cone snail&nbsp;
and in the bite of the blue-ringed octopus.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.840
In addition to the pain-inducing neurotoxin, the&nbsp;
feathers also carry several unidentified stimulant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.960
compounds, which seem to be intended to keep&nbsp;
the victim awake for as long as possible. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.680
the neurotoxin on its own would induce enough&nbsp;
pain for the affected party to pass out almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.680
immediately, the stimulants serve&nbsp;
to keep the victim conscious.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.720
SCP Foundation scientists posit that&nbsp;
this is so that 469 can get as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.000
noise, in this case in the form&nbsp;
of screams, out of its prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.840
as possible and achieve maximum growth before&nbsp;
the captive creature dies or goes into shock.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.680
Further experiments confirmed this theory, and&nbsp;
showed that SCP 469 will react similarly when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:24.720
exposed to any living creature capable of making&nbsp;
a sound and experiencing pain, not just humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.160
Nonliving matter though, including dead&nbsp;
animals, elicit no response from 469.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.400
Additional experiments were then undertaken&nbsp;
that involved applying various different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:40.000
sounds in order to test their effect on&nbsp;
SCP 469’s growth. Though it feeds on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.440
sounds produced in its presence, it seems&nbsp;
to respond most strongly to musical sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.480
exhibiting a particularly strong response&nbsp;
to classical music. No sound elicits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.520
a stronger reaction from this creature,&nbsp;
however, than the sound of ringing bells.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.200
It is rumored that when a bell was&nbsp;
rung in the presence of the 469,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.280
the humanoid at the center of the feathers&nbsp;
is said to have moved for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.640
on record. Apparently whatever is at the center&nbsp;
appeared to “wake up” and unfurl its wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.000
revealing itself. But unfortunately, all&nbsp;
security footage of this incident has been wiped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.960
and the data has been expunged from the record&nbsp;
leaving the creature’s true form a mystery.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.200
You may be remembering that four members of&nbsp;
Foundation personnel on record were lost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.840
SCP-469, and that only two have been mentioned&nbsp;
so far. The second pair of casualties resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.240
when the Foundation attempted to terminate SCP&nbsp;
469. Believing that there was no more scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.520
benefit to keeping it alive, or at least that&nbsp;
the possible benefits did not outweigh the risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.560
it was ordered that SCP-469 be&nbsp;
terminated by any means necessary.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:52.480
Two skilled personnel, Dr. Jones and Dr. Smith,&nbsp;
were sent into the containment facility in hazmat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.520
gear, armed with several sharp instruments.&nbsp;
They were to attempt to use these to cut through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:02.080
the forest of feathers and soft bones until&nbsp;
they could reach the humanoid at the center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.680
It was presumed that once they did&nbsp;
and the creature no longer had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.400
protection of its poisonous feathers,&nbsp;
that it would be relatively easy to kill.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.720
They could not have been more wrong.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.760
Dr. Jones approached the dense cover of feathers&nbsp;
with a pair of sharpened gardening shears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.400
while Dr. Smith opted for a machete. At&nbsp;
first, the strategy appeared to be working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.600
Dr. Jones made several quick cuts, with feathers&nbsp;
fluttering to the floor and sticking to the Hazmat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:34.080
suit as Dr. Smith slashed into the feathers with&nbsp;
his machete, making similarly promising progress.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.960
However, the situation quickly took a turn for&nbsp;
the worse when Dr. Jones dropped her shears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.280
and let out a bloodcurdling scream. The&nbsp;
feathers had taken a little longer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.720
get through the suit to her skin, but somehow&nbsp;
they had managed to find a way just the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.120
Smith grabbed Jones and attempted to make&nbsp;
a break for it, but he was too slow, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.880
far too close to the feathers already. The wings&nbsp;
wrapped around them and swallowed them both up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.600
leaving the researchers outside helpless to&nbsp;
do anything but listen to their screams and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.080
wait for them to go quiet. After&nbsp;
several minutes they finally did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:14.560
and the now larger SCP-469 had officially&nbsp;
claimed another two Foundation personnel.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.840
Obviously, 469 could not be terminated using any&nbsp;
methods that would place the responsible personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.680
within its grasp. That was simply too risky.&nbsp;
So, the Foundation selected a team of D-class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.640
personnel to attempt to burn the feathers with&nbsp;
an array of flamethrowers. Though the feathers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.600
were vulnerable to fire and began to blacken&nbsp;
and disintegrate on contact, the sound of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:42.720
flames being expelled was loud enough to feed&nbsp;
SCP 469. Its growth was so quick in response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:48.080
the noise that the fire could not keep up with the&nbsp;
amount of new feathers and wings being produced.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.480
By the time the flamethrowers ran&nbsp;
out of propellant, 469 was the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.640
size as it had been when they started. Other&nbsp;
termination methods have been discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:01.280
including the possibility of submerging the&nbsp;
entire creature in a highly corrosive acid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.080
but so far this has not yet been attempted.&nbsp;
Whatever they end up trying, it is clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:11.600
that nothing that produces a significant&nbsp;
amount of noise will be able to kill 469.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.960
So, where is it now? What’s become of this&nbsp;
perverse angel with the never-ending wings?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.160
Currently, it is kept isolated in an&nbsp;
airtight, soundproof chamber where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:26.560
nothing can trigger the growth of any more&nbsp;
wings. So, what is it? An angel? A demon?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.320
A twisted, mutated bird of some kind? It&nbsp;
is entirely possible we will never know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.320
But It’s a Wonderful Life had&nbsp;
something right, even if by accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:43.200
Every time a bell rings, something, twisted&nbsp;
and deadly though it may be, gets its wings.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.640
Now check out “SCP-096 - Look At A Picture Of&nbsp;
the Shy Guy In Space? The Shy Guy Questions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:54.880
Theories” and “SCP-082 - Fernand The Cannibal”&nbsp;
for more Foundation mysteries unraveled!

